    "case_id": "amb1-case02-nonprofit-scale",
    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 1 USER]\ne001\tI direct a small nonprofit that runs evening digital-literacy workshops for adults.\ne002\tA community center across town, Willow Hub, wants us to start a second site in autumn.\ne003\tTheir neighborhood has very little free support, and our mission is access.\ne004\tBut our current site is inconsistent: some nights we have thirty participants, some nights twelve, and volunteer coverage is fragile.\ne005\tI cannot tell whether expansion would force us to become better or expose that the first site is not actually stable.\n[Turn 1 ASSISTANT]\ne006\tExpansion will not automatically create discipline; it can just duplicate uncertainty.\ne007\tBefore committing, I would separate demand, delivery capacity, and what you mean by a successful site.\ne008\tWillow Hub's interest establishes a possible channel, not yet reliable attendance or staffing.\ne009\tIf the current variation comes from scheduling or outreach, a second location might help.\ne010\tIf it comes from an unclear program or exhausted volunteers, another site could magnify it.\n[Turn 7 USER]\ne059\tI am leaning toward accepting a four-month, twice-monthly program at Willow Hub, but only if we recruit one named site coordinator and finish the first version of the drop-in curriculum before launch.\ne060\tI want to tell participants from the beginning that the schedule will be reviewed after four months.\ne061\tMy volunteer lead thinks those conditions are reasonable, although she worries the curriculum requirement will become another delay.\ne062\tI have not decided what happens if we find the coordinator but the materials are only partly ready.\n[Turn 7 ASSISTANT]\ne063\tThat is a conditional direction, not a disguised final answer, and the unresolved point matters.\ne064\tYou could define what “ready enough” means in terms of what a participant and volunteer must be able to do, rather than whether every planned material is finished.\ne065\tBut setting that threshold will still involve judgment, and your colleague may reasonably challenge it.\ne066\tThe most useful next conversation is probably not “are we expanding?”\ne067\tbut “which imperfections are we willing to carry into a time-limited public commitment, and who carries them?”",
